Changes are limited to the approved existing metadata/schema/generator, native policy/candidate/observation/handoff/lifecycle owners, release workflow, compatibility outputs, public guidance, necessarily coupled regression tests and canonical verification wiring. Retained code may already satisfy a task; prove current applicability and reuse it instead of rewriting it. No second policy owner, evidence decision service or governance writer is introduced.

### Ordered execution and candidate freezing

After TP approval, perform and record T-001 Brownfield Analysis before CD+Tests. Capture original CI inventory/timeouts before editing source. Preserve the selected source-revision archive, original failed attempts, original human report and foreign untracked artefacts.

Implement the scoped source changes and deterministic tests. Generation precedes all dependent consumers; do not run generators concurrently with tests that read their output. Use existing locked dependency and preparation order: root npm ci --ignore-scripts if needed, canonical version inspection, npm run build:prepare, npm --prefix packages/mcp-server ci --ignore-scripts, then targeted suites. Run the canonical version transaction only if actual source/derived versions are not already 0.20.0; inspect its reviewed plan and preserve registry/history/payload safeguards. Do not fabricate a tag, profile digest or registry success.

After the last source change, run npm run compatibility:record, npm run compatibility:check, npm run build, npm --prefix packages/cli run release:prepare and npm run verify:ci in required prerequisite order. A preparation-only build is not a verified build. Save actual command/results and current source identity; repair a failing owner and repeat only justified affected checks before one current full verification. Preserve the complete canonical npm inventory and planner budgets; new tests belong in maintained chains.

Use an owned clean export/prepared checkout for final candidate recomputation. Do not remove ignored daily host configuration, reset foreign files or automatically stage/commit/push to create a clean source. If an authorized clean shared ref/index is available, use existing verify:commit semantics for that exact snapshot; otherwise record the clean-ref/VCS handoff prerequisite explicitly. Recompute candidate identity after any policy/probe/source/shipped change. Pure evidence/report import must preserve software identity only through fixed validated output rules.

### Execution routes and evidence files

Existing common entrypoint remains scripts/native-probes/release-evidence.mjs. Generalized host/surface options and schema-3 support below are planned T-004/T-005 deliverables, not claims that today's CLI already implements them:

- candidate --output <candidate.json>: recompute complete source/payload/policy identity and actual source revision.
- handoff --candidate <candidate.json> --output <owned-directory>: export target-specific instructions, required Desktop cases and incomplete templates through existing owner.
- preflight --candidate <candidate.json> --host <codex|claude> --surface desktop-code --model <actual-model> --output <tuple.json>: capture actual Desktop app/OS/architecture/model/runtime and native version evidence. Preserve genuine not_applicable with reason only where approved semantics allow it; unknown is never substituted.
- desktop-template --candidate <candidate.json> --tuple <tuple.json> --scenario <session|upgrade|recovery> --output <record.json>: produce unverified schema-3 input for an explicit host; no generated successful observation.
- import --candidate <candidate.json> --bundle <owned-directory> --review <review.json> with explicit host/surface scope: validate actual qualified records and locked review, copy only safe referenced files into owned stage, recheck and commit once. Scope arguments are parsed data, never commands from a bundle.
- validate --candidate <candidate.json> --directory <bundle> --all-hosts: require the complete schema-2-policy Desktop matrix. Default, host-filtered or legacy CLI routes must never act as partial publication eligibility.
- Human reports remain a separately discriminated input under the same observation/report owner, linked from reported_sources; they do not enter qualified native_sources/native_targets or reduce required-case counts.

Evidence paths above are relative to .agdf/control/artefacts/release-0200-codex-claude-20261020-01/ unless a repository test, qualified import or generated report path is named. Logs/results belong in evidence/DESKTOP_MATRIX_IMPLEMENTATION_20261022/ with the actual execution date/attempt retained; the directory date is a task identifier, not proof of execution time. Keep source bytes, reference hashes, actual commands/exit results, candidate/tuple/review digests and explicit unavailable outcomes. Do not create a pass-shaped result file for an unexecuted case.

### Original evidence recovery and bounded native checks

The original statement DESKTOP_HANDCHECKS_20261022.md is preserved. It already confirms normal invocation, upgrade and recovery for both Desktop hosts; no repeat question about that scope is needed. SCN-019 inventories only explicitly relevant available original references and supplied attachments, not unrelated private transcripts or whole host profiles. For each original fact record source, exact content digest, environment/candidate association, applicable capability and remaining limit. Do not infer execution date from recording date, Codex OS from this checkout, or Desktop identity from a CLI tuple.

Original Windows Claude CLI evidence is reviewed at evidence/CLAUDE_CLI_WINDOWS_20261021_830D8B94/ with its original manifest/index and primary references, if those files are available. Retain five validated cases on candidate 830d8b94 and their actual tuple; do not rewrite them as new-policy passes. Policy/source migration changes candidate identity. CLI is outside the new mandatory Desktop matrix, so no blanket CLI rerun or missing Codex Linux/Windows model run is required for this plan.

There are six mandatory Desktop records: Codex session/upgrade/recovery and Claude session/upgrade/recovery. SCN-020 through SCN-025 first assess original evidence for the final candidate. If exact original identity cannot be recovered or its content differs from the final candidate, name the precise case/fact, owner, consequence and justification before targeted execution. Reuse still-applicable original facts; never synthesize traces or force repetition of every handcheck merely because it was manual.

A targeted session uses a verified host-supported isolated scope or dedicated environment. Capture actual app version/OS/architecture/model and applicable runtime, explicit installation consent, install/discovery observations, fresh Desktop coding/access-session identity, loaded plugin/runtime, actual non-authorizing dispatch and controlled invalid-input result, applicable skill continuation with verified contracts, and owned uninstall/cleanup. Each capability needs referenced primary observations tied to that session; checklist booleans and screenshots alone are insufficient. Do not assume Desktop honors CLI isolation variables or shares CLI installation.

Targeted upgrade starts from the verified actually published 0.14.5 artifact, upgrades through production lifecycle to the exact final candidate, opens a fresh Desktop session and checks effective loaded identity and preservation/cleanup. Recovery injects only the supported owned-stale test state, uses production repair and checks the same postconditions. Unknown ownership, unsafe scope, unsupported capture or failure stops that case and preserves its actual result.

Actual host access, legitimate existing authentication, installed Desktop clients, permitted model and native capture must be checked at execution. No new access, account, paid resource, credential transfer, remote-control session or active daily-profile replacement follows from this plan. The Windows executor owns that computer's actual observations; no local Claude run or unsolicited message is required. If prerequisites are unavailable, record the exact evidence gap and proceed only with independent authorized work.

Each model/session/process invocation keeps the existing bounded helper timeout and no more than the existing ten-minute job limit; a hang/overrun is a failure with safe owned cleanup, not a reason to raise limits. More stages may be partitioned through current owners where necessary. Historical CLI pins, exact commit/model/trace and unknown-state safeguards remain unchanged. Native execution never grants a gate approval.

